 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcLx3roxgA6/",
 "title": "Stand out in meetings using the closing technique",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "This video provides a simple strategy for quiet or shy individuals to stand out in meetings without talking constantly. By summarizing action items in the last two minutes, you project authority and leadership.",
 "key_points": [
  "Most people who want to get noticed assume they need to talk a lot and get in early.",
  "The creator calls the alternative strategy the closing technique.",
  "Pay attention to the clock and speak up in the last two minutes of the meeting.",
  "Ask to nail down next steps before breaking, such as confirming who is doing the budget revision and scheduling client calls.",
  "Turning a discussion into a plan shows a rare level of responsibility and competence.",
  "You do not need to do most of the talking or make a big presentation to make an impact."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Dorie Clark | Personal Branding, Communication, Leadership. Text / caption: You don’t have to be the loudest voice in the room to stand out in a meeting. Even if you’re typically quiet or shy, this 90 second trick can help you make an impression at work. I taught communication at Duke University for 10+ years and this small technique can help. Follow me for more on building the career you want. Transcript: Here is how to stand out in a meeting even if you're typically the quiet one. There is one thing you can say at the end of almost any meeting that gets noticed and shows authority regardless of what your actual title is. I taught communication and leadership at Duke and Columbia and this is one of the simplest moves I know for getting noticed at work. Most people who want to get noticed in meetings assume they need to talk more. You've got to get in early and weigh in on everything. But talking a lot mostly proves that you like talking, and it's hard for people who might consider themselves shy or introverts. So instead, try what I call the closing technique. Meetings tend to end the same way. Time runs out and everyone scatters to the next thing. But here is what to do instead. Pay attention to the clock and in the last two minutes, you say, before we break, can we nail down next steps? What I've heard is that Jen will do the budget revision by Friday, I'm scheduling a client call, and we're deciding on the vendor next week. Did I miss anything? And that is it, 30 seconds. It works because turning a discussion into a plan is what leaders do. So when you do it, the leaders in the meeting start to see you as one of them. It's showing a level of responsibility and competence that is rare, and to make an impact in a meeting, you don't need to do most of the talking or make a big presentation. 30 seconds at the end and you have made the impact you need. Follow me for more on becoming a better communicator. Important text shown on screen: How to stand out in a meeting even if you're the quiet one",
